# docker

🟨 running neard with docker

ℹ️ this guide is based on [near-nodes.io/validator/docker-setup](https://near-nodes.io/validator/docker-setup), and focused primarily on mainnet

---

setup

```sh
# For mainnet
docker pull nearprotocol/nearcore:mainnet

# Initializing the Node
CHAIN_ID=mainnet
NODE_TYPE=validator  # or rpc

mkdir -p ~/.near
docker run --rm -v $HOME/.near:/root/.near nearprotocol/nearcore:$CHAIN_ID neard init --chain-id $CHAIN_ID --download-genesis --download-config $NODE_TYPE
```

👀 set NEAR boot nodes up to sync [near-nodes.io/intro/node-epoch-sync](https://near-nodes.io/intro/node-epoch-sync)

---

## boot nodes from multiple RPCs (better sync)

The default `boot_nodes` field is empty, and a single RPC's `network_info` view only shows ~30–40 viable candidates. Querying **multiple RPCs** and merging their peer lists gives you ~100+ diverse boot nodes — this dramatically improves state sync speed (especially for the "stuck on a few shards" problem) because different RPCs peer into different clusters (regular RPCs, archival nodes, hosted services, etc.).

```sh
# Query multiple RPCs and merge their boot candidates (active peers that are also known producers)
BOOT_NODES=$(for RPC in \
  https://rpc.mainnet.near.org \
  https://free.rpc.fastnear.com \
  https://archival-rpc.mainnet.fastnear.com \
  https://rpc.shitzuapes.xyz \
  https://rpc.intea.rs; do
  curl -s --max-time 20 -X POST "$RPC" \
    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"network_info","params":[],"id":"x"}' \
  | jq -r '.result.active_peers as $list1 | .result.known_producers as $list2 |
      $list1[] as $active_peer | $list2[] |
      select(.peer_id == $active_peer.id) |
      "\(.peer_id)@\(.active_peer.addr)"'
done | sort -u | paste -sd "," -)

# Write to config.json
jq --arg newBootNodes "$BOOT_NODES" '.network.boot_nodes = $newBootNodes' \
  ~/.near/config.json > ~/.near/config.tmp \
  && mv ~/.near/config.json ~/.near/config.json.backup \
  && mv ~/.near/config.tmp ~/.near/config.json

echo "boot_nodes count: $(jq '.network.boot_nodes | split(",") | length' ~/.near/config.json)"
```

📋 as of August 2026 this returns ~110 unique boot nodes.

### Why this matters

- **Regular RPCs** (e.g. `rpc.mainnet.near.org`, `free.rpc.fastnear.com`) see ~30–40 viable peers each, mostly within their own cluster.
- **Archival RPCs** (e.g. `archival-rpc.mainnet.fastnear.com`) peer heavily with other archival nodes and keep state parts for **every** epoch (no GC). They're the only source of older parts when shards get stuck.
- **Hosted RPCs** (e.g. `intea.rs`, `shitzuapes.xyz`) peer into yet other clusters, often with low overlap to the official ones.

Merging all of them ensures your node can find a wider set of peers and lift `max_num_peers` is actually saturated.

---

## tune concurrency for faster state sync (advanced)

The default `state_sync.concurrency` is conservative. For a fast host with 8+ CPU cores, bump these:

```json
"state_sync": {
  "sync": "Peers",
  "concurrency": {
    "apply": 8,
    "apply_during_catchup": 1,
    "peer_downloads": 50,
    "per_shard": 12
  },
  "parts_compression_lvl": 1
}
```

Notes:
- `peer_downloads` (default 10) controls concurrent state-part requests in flight. **Ceiling ~50–80** — past that, peers rate-limit you.
- `apply` (default 4) is the trie-reconstruction parallelism. **Ceiling ≈ `cpu_cores - 2`** — raising it further causes RocksDB contention.
- `per_shard` (default 6) is per-shard fairness cap. 12 works well.
- `apply_during_catchup` (default 1) — leave alone, this is when the node is also catching up blocks.

Also raise `network.max_num_peers` so the new boot nodes actually translate into more connections:

```json
"network": {
  "max_num_peers": 60,
  "ideal_connections_lo": 40,
  "ideal_connections_hi": 50,
  "minimum_outbound_peers": 8
}
```

Requires a restart (concurrency is not hot-reloaded).

---

run

```sh
docker run -d \
  --name neard \
  -v $HOME/.near:/root/.near \
  -p 3030:3030 \
  -p 24567:24567 \
  nearprotocol/nearcore:mainnet neard run
```

update

```sh
docker stop neard && docker rm neard
docker pull nearprotocol/nearcore:mainnet
docker run -d \
  --name neard \
  -v $HOME/.near:/root/.near \
  -p 3030:3030 \
  -p 24567:24567 \
  nearprotocol/nearcore:mainnet neard run
```

other

```sh
docker logs -f neard
docker start neard
docker stop neard
docker rm neard

# check how much space it is taking
du -sh ~/.near
```

